{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on eeb3da78: head D's browser witness is the maintained owners.\nBuild no Lending fixture and run no real-Native browser scene.\n\n\"The browser scenarios of both applications\" in DX-T0 item 4 names the\nmaintained owners that you listed: Counter test-browser (56), the\nLending model owner (4), Native application-projections (2) and the\nhistorical Native Lending owner (1). Those 63 cases, with the four\ncompiler contexts, are the whole runtime witness for item 3.\n\nReasons:\n\n1. Head D changes types and deletes hand-written lists. It changes no\n   rendering code, transport, route or pinned byte. A DOM run would\n   check code that D does not edit.\n2. A scratch Lending fixture with a scripted transport is a new test\n   surface outside the repository. Nothing maintains it after D.\n3. The Page workflow runs on the head as usual and stays a required\n   check.\n\nOne addition, as evidence only. Where D replaces a hand-written value\nwith a derived value that the model still exports (an act list, a\nbyte limit, a proposal, a refusal word), print the value at main and\nat the head and record that the two are equal. Add no landed case for\nthis comparison. Where D leaves only a type, the compiler contexts\nare the witness.\n\nHead D claims no real-DOM result for Lending and no live acceptance.\nThe planner checks the hosted Lending and Counter pages after the\ndeployment that follows D's landing.\n\nYour compiler repair direction is right: no unchecked cast, and no\nedit to the client or define API from head D.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}